e equations, assign all carbon sources and sinks, clarify decarboxylation/hydrolysis byproducts, correct for N and H count discrepancies.\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.7,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.7,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.3\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.7,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.95,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.7,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"The formaldehyde\u2013hydroxylamine pathway is literature-consistent (Or\u00f3 et al.) and mostly plausible but several reactions (notably hydrolyses) are unbalanced. The initial steps are chemically solid, but later hydrolysis to glycine is in the same problematic form as Strecker. Needs consistent atom/charge stoichiometry.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\"Correct mass/charge conservation in all steps, ensure formaldoxime and precursor conversion is explicit, add water or other correctants as needed.\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.5\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.5,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.7,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.9,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.6,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.59,\n      \"feedback\": \"Classical formaldehyde + ammonia yields glycine after hydrolysis (Fox & Windsor) but the reaction is unbalanced\u2014missing a carbon unit and short of H and O. Chemistry is plausible in general, but not as written. Mechanism and required hydrolysis are under-specified.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\"Balance the equation, specify hydrolysis byproducts, clarify chemical structure of the intermediate polymer if relevant.\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.45\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.45,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.8,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.7,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.6,\n      \"feedback\": \"Impact shock chemistry is supported by literature (Suzuki et al.) but reaction is unbalanced in elements and no obvious correction with simple species. Chemistry is plausible for impact, but representative as a precursor generation, not clean single-step glycine synthesis.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\"Balance atom and charge, specify nitrogen handling, clarify any intermediates or side products, consider compatibility with both shock and aqueous media.\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.7,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.5\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.6,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.8,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.9,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.7,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.69,\n      \"feedback\": \"Ethanolamine oxidation route follows the Zhang et al. hydrothermal experiment; chemical steps are individually plausible but both reactions are unbalanced (loss of H2 or requirement for O2 unaccounted). Valid route but needs full redox and atom tracking.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\"Balance all equations, add lost H2 for dehydrogenation, clarify oxidant for glycine formation, ensure oxygen source is specified.\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P11\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.5,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.5\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.5,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.7,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 1.0,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.7,\n      \"ove