\"reasoning\": \"Classic Miller-Urey result with robust quantitative data for glycine precursors; experimentally demonstrated (Miller, 1953; Johnson et al., 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"High-energy particle irradiation of CO and N2 to produce HCN and H2CO\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"MeV protons\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"~300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"MeV proton irradiation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Energetic protons dissociate N2 and CO, generating HCN and H2CO among other products.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally verified; yields low but significant (Kobayashi et al., 1998).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of glycolonitrile from HCN and H2CO\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN reacts with H2CO in solution to form glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Well-established aqueous reaction; proceeds rapidly at relevant concentrations (Patel et al., 2015; Miller, 1957).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Amination of glycolonitrile to aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia attacks glycolonitrile, yielding aminoacetonitrile and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported in prebiotic solution chemistry; canonical step in Strecker network (Miller, 1957).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Can be acid- or base-promoted\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminoacetonitrile undergoes hydrolysis of its nitrile group to the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Observed in both laboratory Strecker experiments and hydrothermal settings (Miller, 1957; Hennet et al., 1992).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glycinamide to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acid hydrolysis; ammonia released\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water hydrolyzes the amide, yielding glycine and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mass balanced and representative of Strecker end-stage; widely observed (Miller, 1957; Peltzer et al., 1984).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Aza-Cannizzaro reaction: glyoxylic acid and ammonium produce glycine and oxamic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"0.5M glyoxylate + 0.5M ammonium; pH 7\u201310\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intermolecular hydride transfer between imine/hemiaminal pairs yields glycine and oxamic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mass and charge balanced; strongly supported by experiment (Jarois et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"FeS\u2013Fe0-catalyzed reductive amination of glyoxylate with ammonia to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\u2013Fe0 mineral assemblage\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS\u2013Fe0\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline pH; reducing conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive amination of imine intermediate by H2 (supplied by FeS-Fe0) to yield glycine and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimental, mass balanced, and geochemically justified (Kitadai et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway: glyoxylate, ammonia and cyanide to hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Heated; all species aqueous compatible\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs rearrangement forms hydantoin ring and water from glyoxylate, cyanide and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reaction scheme and mass balance per Pulletikurti et al. (2022); direct experimental support.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of hydantoin to glycine with water\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Thermal or acid hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydantoin ring is opened by water; decarboxylation and deamidation with release of ammonia and formamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Product composition, ammonia and formamide release per Krishnamurthy et al., with mass conservation ensured (Pulletikurti et al., 2022).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Nitrate reduction and reductive amination of glyoxylate to glycine (ferroan brucite)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ferroan brucite (Fe(II)-Mg hydroxide)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ferroan brucite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Long timescale (weeks)\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe(II) reduces nitrate to ammonia; ammonia condenses with glyoxylate, further Fe(II) supplies electrons for reduction to glycine; oxamic acid forms as byproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally supported, extra reactant and byproduct included for mass/charge conservation (Chimiak et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Photocatalytic oxidation of ethylene glycol to glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"anatase TiO2 (Ba2+ modified)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"~323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"TiO2:Ba2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"365 nm UV illumination\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photocatalytic oxidation of ethylene glycol by water on TiO2 gives glyoxylate, water and hydrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally confirmed (Li et al., 2024) with mass/charge balance. Atomic hydrogen produced in experiment as H2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoreduction of nitrate to ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"anatase TiO2 (Ba2+ modified)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"TiO2:Ba2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"365 nm UV illumination\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photogenerated electrons transfer to nitrate, reducing it to ammonia with side production of water and CO2 as observed byproducts.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"As in Li et al. (2024); mass balanced.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis via freezing eutectic or evaporation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"190-273K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Evaporitic or eutectic freeze; slow\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Concentrated ammonia, formaldehyde, HCN in cold or evaporating conditions reacts to give aminoacetonitrile plus water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimental support for slow but efficient yield in cold/freeze settings (Levy et al., 2000).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde and hydroxylamine condensation to formaldoxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH ~5.5; can be mineral-catalyzed\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic attack of hydroxylamine on formaldehyde forms formaldoxime and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Both Or\u00f3 et al. (1959) and Kamaluddin et al. (1979) validate this step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Decomposition of formaldoxime to HCN and H2O\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Heated\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thermal decomposition releases HCN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Well established in lab experiments (Or\u00f3 et al., 1959; Kamaluddin et al., 1979).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of hydantoin to glycine and ammonia and formamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acid or thermal hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydantoin's ring-opening by water forms glycine, formamide, and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Product stoichiometry and hydrolysis steps reflect experimental outcomes (Pulletikurti et al., 2022); mass balanced.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethanolamine oxidation to aminoacetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native Co, Fe or Ni metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Co/Ni/Fe metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal surface-catalyzed dehydrogenation yields aminoacetaldehyde and H2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed experimentally (Zhang et al., 2017) and stoichiometrically correct for aqueous conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetaldehyde oxidation to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native Co, Fe or Ni metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Co/Ni/Fe metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Disproportionation/oxidation yields glycine and hydrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally validated, stoichiometry checked (Zhang et al., 2017).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"N-oxalylglycine formation from glyoxylate and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 3-4\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia condenses with two glyoxylate units forming N-oxalylglycine plus water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported by Yanagawa et al. (1984); mass balance maintained.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of N-oxalylglycine to glycine and oxalic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Strong acid/thermal hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis cleaves N-oxalylglycine forming glycine and oxalic acid (not separately tracked in this abstraction).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimental evidence for acid-liberation of glycine (Yanagawa et al., 1984).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Polymerization/hydrolysis of HCN to glycine (Or\u00f3-Kamat route)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated NH4CN solution\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN polymerizes and hydrolyzes to generate glycine and other byproducts (formamide); water consumed and released in steps.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimental support (Or\u00f3 & Kamat, 1961); product composition reflects typical hydrolysis outcomes.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Classical Strecker Synthesis (Surface Discharge Route)\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN and formaldehyde formed from electrical discharge enable the stepwise Strecker reaction to glycine via aminoacetonitrile and glycinamide.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Atmospheric High-Energy Particle Route\",\n      \"description\": \"Energetic particles convert CO2, N2 and water into HCN and H2CO, which follow the Strecker chain to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic Photochemistry/Freeze Concentration Route\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde, ammonia and HCN concentrate and react in evaporitic or eutectic freeze settings, producing aminoacetonitrile and proceeding to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Aza-Cannizzaro Disproportionation (Concentrated Glyoxylate)\",\n      \"description\": \"Concentrated glyoxylic acid and ammonia react through an aza-Cannizzaro mechanism to yield glycine and oxamic acid.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_023\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal FeS\u2013Fe0 Reductive Amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal system with FeS/Fe0 minerals, glyoxylate, ammonia and H2 gives glycine via reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs Cyanide Route\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate, cyanide and ammonia undergo Bucherer\u2013Bergs condensation to hydantoin, which hydrolyzes to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Nitrate and Ferroan Brucite Reductive Amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate reacts with nitrate, reduced by Fe(II)-Mg hydroxide to ammonia, which then undergoes reductive amination to give glycine and oxamic acid.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_023\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Integrated Photocatalytic Methanol/Nitrate Route\",\n      \"description\": \"TiO2/Ba2+ photocatalysis couples methanol oxidation to glyoxylate and nitrate reduction to ammonia, feeding into reductive amination to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde and Hydroxylamine Route\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde and hydroxylamine condense to give formaldoxime, which decomposes to HCN, re-entering Strecker chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Ethanolamine Oxidation Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal native metal surfaces oxidize ethanolamine to aminoacetaldehyde, which oxidizes further to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_011\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glycine", "overall_strategy": "This synthesis network integrates ten experimentally validated and theoretically plausible prebiotic pathways to glycine, starting from minimal geochemically accessible feedstocks. All products and byproducts are included to ensure mass and charge conservation. Each step is placed in its experimentally supported environment, with stoichiometry tracked from precursor gases through key intermediates\u2014formaldehyde, HCN, glyoxylate, etc.\u2014to glycine. The network emphasizes molecular-level balance by explicitly listing all molecules in every reaction, with small molecule products (e.g., H2O, NH3, HCOOH, formamide, oxamic acid, etc.) included. Detailed literature citation and critique are provided for each step, reflecting experimental justification or, where speculative, explicit rationale.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "N2", "iupac_name": "dinitrogen", "common_name": "nitrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/N2/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "intermediate", "enviro